      "text": "To solve for the vector at \\( t = -7 \\), we need to first determine the parametric equations of the line in terms of \\( t \\). The line is parameterized by a parameter \\( t \\), so we can express the coordinates as functions of \\( t \\). We are given two points on the line:\n\n- At \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n- At \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nThe direction vector can be found by determining the change in \\( t \\) and the corresponding change in the coordinates. The increase in \\( t \\) from 2 to 3 is \\( 1 \\) and the difference in the \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) coordinates is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\). Therefore, the direction vector can be taken as \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nThis means for each increase of 1 in \\( t \\), the coordinates change as follows: \\( x \\) increases by 2 and \\( y \\) decreases by 8. Thus, the parametric equations for the line can be written as:\n\\[ x = 1 \\cdot t + 1 \\]\n\\[ y = 4 \\cdot t + 4 \\]\nor in vector form:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\):\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} x' \\\\ y' \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-7) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nCalculate each component separately:\n\\[ x' = 1 + (-7) \\cdot 2 = 1 - 14 = -13 \\]\n\\[ y' = 4 + (-7) \\cdot (-8) = 4 + 56 = 60 \\]\nSo, the vector at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} x' \\\\ y' \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}} \\]",
