      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the direction vector of the line first. Let's denote the direction vector by $\\mathbf{d}$.\n\nGiven the vectors at two different points on the line:\n- When $t=2$, the vector is $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$\n- When $t=3$, the vector is $\\mathbf{v}_3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$\n\nThe direction vector $\\mathbf{d}$ can be found by subtracting the vector at $t=2$ from the vector at $t=3$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = \\mathbf{v}_3 - \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo, the parametric equation of the line can be written as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\mathbf{r}_0 + t \\mathbf{d}\n\\]\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}_0$ is the vector at $t=0$. From the given information, when $t=0$, the vector is $\\mathbf{v}_0 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} - 0 \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$. Therefore, the parametric equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2t \\\\ 4 - 8t \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the vector on the line at $t=-7$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(-7) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2(-7) \\\\ 4 - 8(-7) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 14 \\\\ 4 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo, the vector on the line at $t=-7$ is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}}$. Let's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors at t=2 and t=3\nv2 = np.array([1, 4])\nv3 = np.array([3, -4])\n\n# Direction vector\nd = v3 - v2\n\n# Parametric equation of the line\ndef r(t):\n    return v2 + t * d\n\n# Find the vector at t=-7\nvector_t_minus_7 = r(-7)\nprint(vector_t_minus_7)\n```"